 "drivingQuestion": "May the laborer return to the employer if the shopkeeper fails to pay?",
 "outcome": "inconclusive",
 "outcomeNote": "Two amoraic answers; argued in the next unit",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_metzia.112a.7",
   "daf": "112a",
   "sentenceId": 6,
   "he": "הִמְחָהוּ אֵצֶל חֶנְוָנִי וְאֵצֶל שׁוּלְחָנִי – אֵינוֹ עוֹבֵר. אִיבַּעְיָא לְהוּ: חוֹזֵר, אוֹ אֵינוֹ חוֹזֵר? רַב שֵׁשֶׁת אָמַר: אֵינוֹ חוֹזֵר, וְרַבָּה אָמַר: חוֹזֵר.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «If» the one who owes the money «transferred his» payment by leaving instructions «with a storekeeper or with a money changer» to pay him, «he does not transgress» the prohibitions. «A dilemma was raised before» the Sages: If the storekeeper or money changer neglected to pay the wages, may the laborer «return» to the employer and claim his money from him, «or» may he «not return,» as the storekeeper or money changer is now his exclusive address for complaints? «Rav Sheshet says he may not return, and Rabba says he may return.»"
  }
 ],
